A||||30||Nashco||Anybody into RC stuff have any spare parts?||||1||
Z||000000||Nashco||10-13-2001||07:55 PM||||My friend at work is playing with a project and I think it's pretty cool.  He had this idea of putting a nitro RC airplane motor into an RC car.  He's using a KB .61 (I think) motor and grafting it into a RC10 car.  He knows the .21 can have about the same power capabilities, but the .61 has more torque.  He's using a one speed trans and wants the car to be able to take off and hopefully peak out a little above 100 mph.  We're not quite sure how the vehicle dynamics will be yet, but that's all in the works.<P>Anyway, his low buck project is potentially getting too expensive.  You see, he got the motor as a gift, the car chassis as a gift, and somebody is loaning him their radio.  I was hoping maybe somebody had some parts laying around they'd be willing to donate.  Just about anything is usable, as we're both very resourceful.  Some stuff we know would be useful is:<P>Micro steering servos (2, one for steering, one for throttle)<BR>Radio Receiver (will pay some, just trying to keep expenses down)<BR>Any aerodynamic body (1:10 scale)<BR>Any stuff like glue, fuel, tires, etc. that are always in need<BR>Some quality suspension parts would be awesome!<BR>Fuel Tanks (any shape/size, we're experimenting with weight, trying to get our center of gravity as low as possible with still decent inertia properties)<P>I'm not expecting a ton of support..but it would be awesome if somebody had something laying around they'd be willing to contribute.  So far he's got about 40 bucks into it for wheels, tires, fuel, a prop (for bench running the motor), and a new transmission gear.  He's running very low gears, so the thing should hit 100 as long as we don't have too much drag.<P>I think a radio receiver would be one of the most beneficial parts, so he wouldn't have to borrow one and could use it forever to constantly tweak the car and make it better.  As it is right now it sounds like he'll be able to get the car running, play with it for a week, then he has to give the receiver back....which would totally suck.<P>He keeps talking about getting a tuned pipe that would give him a lot more power, but I have to convince him to wait until he gets the car running, then make it better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Thanks in advance!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>Edited with a couple updates.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||10-14-2001||07:06 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||10-14-2001||07:55 PM||||Um...I don't think I have what you're looking for, but who knows. I have a few R/C cars that don't work anymore. How big is 1:14 scale?<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">            <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*SOLD*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||10-15-2001||01:19 AM||||I actually flubbed that up and need to fix it.  It's actually 1:10.  I figure the chassis is about 12 inches long.  Body and all, figure about 13 or 14 inches long.<P>Like I said, any and all stuff would be appreciated greatly.  This is basically a project for us to see how cheap we can be and still have a very high quality car.  Smaller cars would be nice for some things, as we are limited on space.  An example would be a throttle servo motor, which would more than likely be the steering servo on a smaller car.  So like I said, any and all help is accepted <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I talked with him for a while tonight about it, looking at different options.  It is so fun for me to try and wrap my head around something to try and figure all the effects of doing things and decide the best route to go, or the best route that's still feasible, in this case <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||10-15-2001||02:29 AM||||You said it was a RC10, but that could describe anything from an electric touring car to a nitro powered stadium truck. I'm guessing it's a car, but I don't know if it's 2wd (guessing that it is) or 4, gas or electric (guessing electric too). How did you go about attaching the motor to the car?<BR>A friend of mine made a Traxxas Rad2 into a nitro powered car and basically had to rebuild the whole thing from scratch. The framerails were 2 pieces of aluminum L channel and the car was pretty laughable, but he did make it work. (Then again, he also made a v8 Ford Ranger without a kit and built it for about 700 bucks including the price of the truck and motor. It was cobbled together but it sure did move.) If you make this work, The same guy has a similar RC plane motor that I might have to get from him.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||10-15-2001||02:57 AM||||Thanks for straightening me up Pontiaddict...I should have given a little more detail.<P>It was previously a 2wd electric car.  It appears to have been set up for perhaps a buggy style suspension and prerunner style front and rear end.  We're now using, as I said, a K&B nitro motor.<P>We've obtained some scrap UHMW poly from Gerald at  <A HREF="http://www.8shark.com" TARGET=_blank>www.8shark.com</A>  who has some awesome fiero suspension stuff, by the way.  He is currently making my upper front control arm bushings.  We plan on using that to mount the motor.  I'm not sure how it will hold up to the heat, but we can always shield it from the heat and still use it.  The orientation of the motor is still up in the air.  He wants to mount it vertically (head on top) for airflow reasons, but I'm trying to convince him he can lay it sideways and direct air to it, as it's being used for mostly entirely high speed (50+ mph) stuff.<P>Right now, this is my thinking.  The frame is plenty sturdy, as it has a solid, one piece design, chassis with sides.  Similar to, say, the c-channel through the center of the fiero, the sides provide a lot of strength.  He's using two gear sets between the motor and the transmission to get the gearing he wants.  I want to lay the motor sideways to drop the engine as low as possible, and wrap a fuel cell inside the chassis around the motor.  This should provide for the best center of gravity, and most of the weight close to it.  He wants to have the motor mounted vertically and put fuel cells on each side of the outside of the frame rails.  My way would provide a car that was easier more agile, but perhaps squirelly.  His design provides for a more stable car at high speeds, but one that would be harder to bring back to control if you lose it.<P>It should be fun, and very fast.  The biggest challenge, it appears, will be fitting everything inside a standard body and still providing adequate flow.  He is having a temporary lack of direction and wants to make it a supercar, but I have to keep in mind the "low buck project" aspect and work with what we've got <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I am getting into fiberglassing in hopes of moving up to carbon fiber.  I will be experimenting with making an upper frame setup to make everything more rigid, once we have the components all hard mounted.  I will also probably be making a custom body with airflow straight to the head and exhaust areas with still good aerodynamics, once we have it all built and operational.  It would be really cool to use a few thermocouples and some data acquisition stuff to find out which way works better <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Maybe we could borrow something from work for the weekend some time *thoughts flashing through head*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||10-15-2001||03:21 PM||||WOAH MAN!!!! a .60 in an RC10!!! <P>Heheh well think on this a sec. <BR>The reason you have bigger engines in planes is so that you can spin a larger propeller or a greater pitched one to make it go faster. anyway. with a car what you need to do is change the gearing in the trans to get more top end speed. a .15 engine will be way more than enough power to get the job done, unless your trying to get over 100 MPH then a bigger engine may be needed. even still a .15 is reasonable you may lack the bottom end punch and your holmies will be out in front of you but after that engine gets spooled up your going to smoke em in the long run.<BR>Also you will need a custom head. since a car lacks a propeller to blow LOTS of air over the head to keep the engine cool<P>Here is my <A HREF="http://www.boonyer.com/boonyers" TARGET=_blank>R/C Truck</A> site.<BR>If you need some parts for your RC10 go <A HREF="http://www.boonyer.com/pp" TARGET=_blank>here</A> My cousin works in a machine shop and can make ANY RC10 part from aluminum.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000007||sqoach||10-15-2001||04:25 PM||||Oh! I'm definately not going to be any help. My R/C cars are just battery powered, Radio Shack toys. Oh well.||||reg||11
