Z||000023||jlhuber||10-19-2001||12:27 AM||||I've got a couple of whole R/Cs, but no spare parts.  They all run, but I only have 2 controlers for the land vehicles.  Sorry I can't help out.<P>Traxxas: Hawk (2 of these)<BR>         Hawk 2<BR>         Blue Eagle (first one)<BR>         Nitro Hawk <P>Hobby Lobby: Hunter (1/8th 4wd nitro)<P>Unknown: "Strip-Rat" (uncle gave it to me)<P>Goldberg Models: Tiger 2 airplane 72" wingspan (under construction)<P>A friend of mine had a 1/8th scale .21 4wd buggy doin 80mph ...until a wall jumped up right smack dab in front of it.  ouch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>      <BR>If you expect the unexpected, was it really even unexpected in the first place?||||reg||1
Z||000024||filthyscarecrow||10-19-2001||05:18 PM||||i was going to do that once. <BR>i even bought an engine.<P>there are a few things you need to take into consideration:<BR>1. airplane engines don't work well in cars. at all. the average r/c car engine spins about 30,000 rpm. the average airplane engine spins below 20,000. the gearing you would have to put on the car would be astronomical (let's think about it for a sec... 100mph is approx 45m/sec. the wheel diameter is approx 0.07 meters, and it's circumference about 0.21m. the wheel must therefore spin about 205 revolutions to travel 45 meters, and to get a velocity of 45m/sec it must spin that 205 revolutions in  one second. 205 rev/sec=12,300rev/min. that's at the wheels. the highest output engine that K&B makes in .61 spins 14,000rpm adn makes 2hp. so if we take 1/(wheel rpm/engine rpm) that gives us a gear ratio of 1.13:1. your clutch will not last long enough for a single run, adn having enough slip to get the car moving will most likely result in the clutch never fully engaging. slip=heat and power loss. ) the r/c car that nitro magazine made go 100+mph was geared a LOT lower than that, it used high rpm to achieve top speed. if you're planning on driving the wheels, you'll have to settle for about 60mph out of that engine. i have an HPI nitro RS4 and it goes faster than that with a .15. if you want to go 100+, i suggest you use a prop to drive it. make it a fan car. less drive ****  to worry about (still have to have brakes, mind you), and it will be drag limited, not rpm limited. then start working on aero stuff. <BR>  i really dno't mean to burst your bubble, i mean i thought of doing it too, but when you do the math, it just doesn't work out to be possible. i guess you could use model rocket engines to get it moving... keep in mind though that whatever you build will need assloads of space to get goign that fast too, adn whatever it hits, it will destroy (itself included). good luck...<P>had to recheck my math...<p>[This message has been edited by filthyscarecrow (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Nashco||10-19-2001||06:14 PM||||Ok...I haven't even thought to do the math, as my friend is VERY thorough and has done some massive calculations.  He's even accounted for rolling and aerodynamic resistance.<P>I'll have to talk to him about it tonight.  I haven't considered clutch stuff, I am more focusing on the best package organization.  I know he is using a couple sets of gears to gear it all down, but I can't see that changing what you're stating.  No matter how many gears you have doing it, the overall ratio should be the same as what you said, right?<P>I'm not into the rc stuff much yet, but I have a hard time believing there are no clutches that won't work.  From what I understand, they use centrifugal clutches, like a snowmobile would.  Once they get to a specific RPM, the clutches start to rotate against the housing and grab on...right?  If this is so, couldn't you use a clutch that actuates at very low RPM, to minimize slippage to get it rolling?  I also imagine that the tires allow a certain amount of slippage, as well...are there no clutches that would, say, exceed the traction force of the tires, thus spinning the tires when too much power is available, rather than the clutch?<P>Like I said, I'll talk to him and see what his thoughts behind this were; I guarantee he has some reasoning behind it...he's thorough..or anal..one of those <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Thanks for the other viewpoint filthy...any suggestions are appreciated!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P><BR>||||reg||1
