Z||000027||Nashco||10-20-2001||02:51 AM||||For the record....Cooter is on the A-list <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  How 'bout a round of applause? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Greg, you should have heard my buddy...it's like he was in RC heaven.  Thanks a ton!<P>My buddy has come up with an AWESOME clutch solution...more on that later, after we're done though.  After you see it you will wonder why you hadn't thought of it...trust me, not complex, just amazingly purposeful and simple.<P>My7Fieros: Still waiting to hear back on the airplane motors...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000028||Cooter||10-20-2001||05:13 PM||||Thanks- now I have the $$$$ to send Formula on his intake and carb from his stroker project.||||reg||1
Z||000029||filthyscarecrow||10-21-2001||06:10 PM||||the only issue i see with the clutch is that it has to slip- a lot- in order to get that much weight movign from a standstill with that final drive ratio. the problem lies in that the engine doens't make peak power til probably about 10-12K rpm. thus, engaging the clutch sooner would cause it to bog down, maybe even stall. so the clutch not only has to slip quite a bit, but it has to engage at high RPM. that leaves a very fine line to walk between fully engaging and not. i'd really like to hear the clutch idea he has. <BR>the other issue is that most racing clutches for R/C cars are the centrifugal type, but are made for the RPM of racing engines- twice as many revs as that .61 has. you could abandon the centrifugal clutch altogether and use a belt and tensioner. if it was set to engage fully at full throttle, and slip at, say, 50% throttle, you could just hook the clutch lever to the throttle servo. as you give it gas, it engages. this is the simplest method i can think of, but like i said before, slipping=heat. plan having a lot of spare clutch parts. ||||reg||1
Z||000030||Nashco||10-21-2001||09:39 PM||||Like I said filthy, I'm almost positive it will work, but it will be a while before we get it fabricated and tested.<P>I think we'll have it fabricated in a few weeks.  I'll let you know how we did it then.  If you REALLY want to know, PM and I'll explain it to ya.  I doubt anybody else cares..hehe.  All of the things that you mentioned that we have to overcome are taken care of with this design.  As I said before, it's so simple, you'll wonder why you didn't think of it before <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
